INT. HOTEL ASTORIA SWITCHBOARD ROOM - NIGHT (1927)

A narrow room behind the lobby. A tall brass switchboard glows with a hundred tiny bulbs. A desk lamp, a half-eaten sandwich, a cup of cocoa going cold.

ADELAIDE PRUITT-ROSS, 20s, a headset over her marcelled waves, plugs a cable into a jack with practiced calm. She hums. A BELLHOP named FLOYD, 17, lingers in the doorway, hat in hand, watching her.

FLOYD
Miss Adelaide, the gentleman in 412 wants his bags brought up and his brandy brought down.

ADELAIDE
That's the wrong way round.

FLOYD
That's what he said.

ADELAIDE
Then he's had too much brandy.

She plugs another cable. A bulb winks out. Floyd steps into the room to see better, bumps the desk, and the cocoa cup slides to the edge. Adelaide catches it without looking, and in the same motion her other hand flicks a switch. A voice crackles on the line.

CLARENCE (O.S.)
(over phone)
Operator? Operator, I'd like the front desk, please.

ADELAIDE
(smooth)
One moment, sir. Connecting.

She presses a key. The voice is cut off. She waits a beat, then removes her headset and sets it on the desk.

FLOYD
Who was that?

ADELAIDE
Nobody. A man asking for the front desk.

FLOYD
Isn't that your job?

ADELAIDE
Tonight, it's my pleasure to be difficult.

She takes a bite of the sandwich. Floyd stares. Then the board lights up all at once, every bulb at once, a Christmas tree of demand. Adelaide stands. The headset is back on her head before she has finished chewing.

ADELAIDE (CONT'D)
(into the mouthpiece)
Hotel Astoria, how may I direct your call?

CLARENCE (O.S.)
(over phone)
Miss, I am the man in 412 and I would like it noted that I have been cut off three times.

ADELAIDE
Noted, sir.

She flips a second switch with her elbow. A new voice cuts in, dry and tired.

HOLCOMB (O.S.)
(over phone)
Front desk. Mr. Holcomb speaking. Adelaide, is that man still complaining?

ADELAIDE
He is, Mr. Holcomb. Quite warmly.
